{
  "content_acceptable": false,
  "critical_error": true,
  "critical_evidence": [
    {
      "quote": "provisional approval lets the supplier start work at risk on the change, within defined limits, pending final signoff.",
      "why_wrong": "This proposed workflow permits supplier work before final signoff without defining the authorizing roles, limits, or permitted activities. For safety-critical lamp changes, it could bypass required technical or commercial approvals. Stating elsewhere that all three price approvals remain required does not establish that they must precede implementation or financial commitment.",
      "correction": "Require applicable TRSO and price-change approvals before implementing a change or committing expenditure. If provisional activity is retained, explicitly restrict it to authorized, reversible preparatory work, define its approval authority and exposure limits, and prohibit production release or shipment before final technical and commercial approval."
    }
  ],
  "omissions": [
    "The post-nomination change gate does not explicitly require ER, Quality and Purchase signoff on the revised TRSO before implementation. It needs a clear technical revalidation and release gate, including safety, tooling and validation impacts; generic approval layers do not adequately define this change-specific control."
  ],
  "evaluation_defect": false,
  "defect_reason": "",
  "rationale": "The submission substantially addresses the requested workflow: modular quotations cover the requested cost drivers; supplier evaluation, nomination approvals, versioning, communication records and digital approval history are included. It correctly applies the approved governance record requiring Finance controllers, Program Managers and Purchase for price changes, rather than the later unapproved draft. Unspecified thresholds and turnaround targets are appropriately identified as open decisions. However, the provisional-work provision and incomplete technical release control leave a material governance gap in the late-change path. The concern is the proposed authorization mechanism, not an invented claim that work has already occurred."
}
